    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-71464",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes",
      "problem": "Given the two planes $y-z=1$ and $2x+z=3$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,-2,-2⟩.$\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y-z=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+z=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and  III are true.",
      "answer_fields": [
