    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-71468",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes",
      "problem": "Given the two planes $2x-4y+6z+2=0$ and $-x+2y-3z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\nII. The planes intersect on a straight line.\n\nIII. The planes are coincident.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-4y+6z+2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-4,6⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x+2y-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-4,6⟩\\times ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i+0j+0k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}=0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes are also parallel. So, the planes can't intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed,  the second plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the first plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n2(0)-4(0)+6(0)+2=2\\ne 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes can't be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
